caption: Prototype Skill and Margin
technology-rule: prototype-skill-and-margin

Unless the designer’s actual skill is specified, use the following Raw Skill:

Talent 0: 14.

Talent 1: 16.

Talent 2: 20.

Talent 3 or higher: 8 × Talent.

These benchmark skills use Actual Talent, not Effective Talent. They are defaults for unspecified or off-screen designers, not caps; a character with a recorded design skill uses that actual skill.

For Actual Talent 5 or higher, the benchmark assumes Will at least 5 × Actual Talent and at least 2 × Actual Talent purchased levels in the specialized design skill above its controlling attribute. A designer who does not meet those assumptions uses their actual skill instead.

Determine complexity from DPL, treating DPL as the formula’s unmodified dollar cost before the Prototype roll or Cost Refinement:

Simple (DPL up to 100): -6.

Average (DPL 101 to 10,000): -10.

Complex (DPL 10,001 to 1,000,000): -14.

Amazing (DPL over 1,000,000): -22.

A good laboratory gives +4. Apply other relevant invention modifiers normally.

Effective Prototype Skill = Raw Skill + Complexity Modifier + Laboratory Modifier + Other Modifiers

Make the Prototype roll. The formula’s invention margin is:

M = Effective Prototype Skill - Prototype Roll

Resolve the Concept roll normally. It determines whether the theory is sound but does not affect cost. A successful Prototype roll produces the reproducible formula. The Prototype roll’s margin, not the Concept roll, fixes the formula’s Cost Factor.
